          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does type 2 diabetes affect blood sugar in the first place?",
          "answer": "Type 2 diabetes affects blood sugar levels due to two main problems, as described in the General clinical reference (educational summary):\n\nFirst, the body's tissues become less responsive to insulin. This is called insulin resistance. Insulin normally helps sugar (glucose) enter cells to be used for energy. With insulin resistance, glucose has a harder time getting into the cells.\n\nSecond, because the body's tissues are not responding well to insulin, the pancreas tries to make more insulin to compensate. However, over time, the pancreas cannot keep up with this increased demand and cannot produce enough insulin to overcome the resistance.\n\nThe combination of insulin resistance and the pancreas's inability to produce enough insulin leads to blood glucose levels staying higher than the normal range.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
